Chapter 1

The Jacobian Conjecture in
Dimension Two

1.1    Statement of the Main Result
The Jacobian Condition
Let k be an algebraically closed field of characteristic 0. Let

                                  F = (P, Q) : A2k −→ A2k

be a polynomial morphism. The Jacobian matrix of F is
                                                
                                       ∂x P ∂y P
                               DF =                .
                                       ∂x Q ∂ y Q

We assume throughout that
                                       det DF ∈ k × .
   This condition implies that F is everywhere étale. Indeed, the Jacobian criterion for
smoothness implies that F is smooth of relative dimension 0 ([2, Theorem 17.11], [8, III.10]).
Hence F is unramified and flat, and therefore étale ([15, Tag 00U3], [7, IV.17]).

Historical Background
The Jacobian Conjecture was formulated by Keller in 1939 [9]. It asserts that polynomial
maps with constant, nonzero Jacobian determinant are invertible with polynomial inverse.
Despite many partial results and reductions, the conjecture remains open in dimension ≥ 2;
see the survey of Bass–Connell–Wright [1] and the comprehensive account in van den Essen
[16].
    In dimension one, the statement is classical and follows from elementary algebraic
arguments. In higher dimension, the essential difficulty lies in controlling the behavior at
infinity.

Precise Statement
Theorem 1.1 (Jacobian Conjecture in Dimension Two). Let k be an algebraically closed
field of characteristic 0. Let
                               F = (P, Q) : A2k −→ A2k

                                              1
2               CHAPTER 1. THE JACOBIAN CONJECTURE IN DIMENSION TWO

be a polynomial morphism such that
                                         det DF ∈ k × .
Then F is an automorphism of A2k .

Strategy of the Proof
The proof proceeds by passing to the normalized projective graph of F and analyzing the
induced morphism
                                     f : X0 → A2 .
After Stein factorization
                                           f = σ ◦ ρ,
with σ finite, we show:

    1. The morphism σ has no affine branch curve.
    2. Hence σ is finite étale.
    3. Every finite étale cover of A2k is trivial (using comparison with complex topology; see
       [4, Exposé XII], [13, III.4]).
    4. Therefore σ is an isomorphism.
    5. The original morphism F is birational and étale, hence an isomorphism by Zariski
       Main Theorem ([6, Theorem 8.12.6], [11, Theorem 8.2]).

    The geometric core of the argument lies in eliminating possible horizontal divisors at
infinity via valuation-theoretic degeneration. The remaining steps rely on standard results
in algebraic geometry.


1.2      Strategy of the Proof
Overview
We outline the logical architecture of the proof and isolate the places where nontrivial
geometry enters. The argument is organized around the normalized projective graph of F
and the finite morphism appearing in the Stein factorization. Standard tools used repeatedly
are Stein factorization, generic freeness, Zariski’s Main Theorem, basic valuation theory,
and the Jacobian criterion for étaleness; see [8, III.11], [3, IV.8], [7, IV.18], and [17, Ch. I,
§3].

Stage 1: Normalized graph and Stein factorization
We replace F : A2 → A2 by the morphism
                                         f : X0 → A2
obtained from the normalization of the projective graph closure, restricted to the affine
target chart. We then take the Stein factorization
                                           f = σ ◦ ρ,
where ρ has connected fibers and σ is finite; this is standard for proper morphisms of finite
type [8, III.11].
1.2. STRATEGY OF THE PROOF                                                                   3

Stage 2: Degree control (generic flatness and Zariski Main Theorem)
Let d = [k(Y0 ) : k(A2 )] be the function field degree of σ. Generic freeness yields a dense
open S ◦ ⊂ A2 on which σ is finite flat of rank d [10, §14.4]. Separately, since det DF ∈ k × ,
the restriction of f to the open chart corresponding to the source affine plane is étale;
applying Zariski’s Main Theorem to this quasi-finite étale morphism gives, after shrinking
the target, a finite étale restriction of degree d [6, Theorem 8.12.6].

Stage 3: U-HIT (elimination of horizontal boundary curves)
We prove a purely geometric “no-horizontal-boundary” statement: there is no irreducible
curve contained in the boundary X0 \ U whose image in A2 is a curve. The proof is a clean
fiber-length argument over S ◦ ∩ S ◦◦ : on this dense open, the total fiber length of σ equals
d, while the fiber inside U already contributes d points, leaving no room for additional
boundary points. This is the key separation that avoids any global quasi-finiteness claims.

Stage 4: Valuation-theoretic degeneration and rank dichotomy
Assuming for contradiction that σ ramifies over the affine target, one produces a dicritical
divisor D ⊂ X0 mapping dominantly onto a curve. At the generic point of D one works in
the associated DVR and considers the valuation filtration. Passing to the Rees (graded)
degeneration transports dimension to the special fiber. A rank dichotomy (single-character
support) then forces the initial forms to lie in a one-variable Laurent subring, yielding a
multiplicative residue relation um
                                 0 v0 = c adapted to the valuation (i.e. ma + nb = 0). This
                                    n

is the only genuinely deep algebraic step; we present it in full detail and cite standard
commutative algebra for the height/dimension mechanism [2, Ch. 13], [12, §15].

Stage 5: Wedge strictness and contradiction with Keller
From the adapted residue relation, a direct computation of dP ∧ dQ in the DVR shows that
the leading term in the valuation filtration vanishes, forcing a valuation jump and hence

                                    ordD (dP ∧ dQ) > 0.

On the other hand, on the affine chart U ≃ A2 the Keller identity

                                dP ∧ dQ = (det DF ) dx ∧ dy

shows dP ∧ dQ is a unit two-form, hence has order 0 along any divisor meeting U . This
contradicts U-HIT, completing the elimination of affine ramification.

Stage 6: Triviality of finite étale covers of A2
Once σ is shown to be finite étale over A2 , we use the standard fact that A2 admits no
nontrivial finite étale covers in characteristic 0. We present a concise reduction to C and
invoke the comparison between finite étale covers and finite topological coverings (Riemann
existence) [4, Exposé XII], [13, III.4].

Stage 7: Final birational and quasi-finite reduction
Triviality of the étale cover implies σ is an isomorphism, hence f is birational. Since
det DF ∈ k × , the original map F is étale and thus quasi-finite. Zariski’s Main Theorem then
factors F as an open immersion followed by a finite morphism; the finite birational morphism
4              CHAPTER 1. THE JACOBIAN CONJECTURE IN DIMENSION TWO

to the normal surface A2 is an isomorphism [6, Theorem 8.12.6], [17, Corollary (4.C)].
Therefore F is an automorphism.
The only genuinely non-formal input of the proof occurs in Stage 4, where valuation-theoretic
degeneration forces a rank-one structure in the associated graded ring. All other steps
rely solely on standard tools: Stein factorization, generic flatness, étale comparison, and
Zariski’s Main Theorem.
