equal to the degree ([7, IV.17.5.1]). Hence the fiber consists of d distinct reduced points. ■

U-HIT
Theorem 5.3 (U-HIT). There exists no irreducible curve

                                              D ⊂ X0 \ U

such that dim f (D) = 1.

Proof. Assume for contradiction that such a curve D exists. Let

                                           C := f (D) ⊂ A2 .

Since f is proper, C is closed ([8, II.4.8]).
   Because D → C is dominant, for a general closed point s ∈ C we have

                                           D ∩ f −1 (s) ̸= ∅.

     Choose
                                           s ∈ C ∩ S ◦ ∩ S ◦◦ .
5.1. STATEMENT OF U-HIT                                                                  17

This intersection is nonempty since all three sets are dense open.
   For this s we have:

                             length f −1 (s) = d (Lemma 5.1)
                                            

   and

                           length f −1 (s) ∩ U = d (Lemma 5.2).
                                              

   Since U is open in X0 , the scheme-theoretic fiber decomposes as

                    f −1 (s) = f −1 (s) ∩ U ⊔ f −1 (s) ∩ (X0 \ U ) .
                                                                 


   Length is additive on finite disjoint unions of zero-dimensional schemes. Therefore

          length f −1 (s) = length f −1 (s) ∩ U + length f −1 (s) ∩ (X0 \ U ) .
                                                                           


   Substituting the identities above yields

                             d = d + length f −1 (s) ∩ (X0 \ U ) ,
                                                                


hence
                               length f −1 (s) ∩ (X0 \ U ) = 0.
                                                          

   Thus
                                    f −1 (s) ∩ (X0 \ U ) = ∅,
contradicting
                                       D ∩ f −1 (s) ̸= ∅.
   Therefore no such curve D exists.                                                      ■

Remark 5.4 (Pure Fiber-Length Argument). The proof of Theorem 5.3 does not use any
global quasi-finiteness assumption on f or on σ. In particular, no prior control on the
behavior of fibers outside the dense open subsets is required.
   The argument relies solely on:

   • the flat fiber-length identity for σ over S ◦ ,

   • the finite étale fiber structure of f |U over S ◦◦ ,

   • and additivity of length for zero-dimensional schemes.

    Thus U-HIT is a purely fiber-length theoretic separation argument, independent of any
global quasi-finiteness or birationality input. In particular, no structural property of the
Stein factor Y0 beyond finite flatness over S ◦ enters the argument.
