Chapter 11

Triviality of Finite Étale Covers

Theorem 11.1. Let k be an algebraically closed field of characteristic 0. Then

                                          π1t (A2k ) = 0.

Equivalently, every finite étale cover of A2k is trivial.
Proof. Step 1: Reduction to a finitely generated subfield.
    Let Y → A2k be a finite étale morphism. Since the morphism is of finite type, there
exists a subfield k0 ⊂ k finitely generated over Q such that both A2k and Y descend to k0 .
That is, there exists a finite étale morphism

                                           Y0 → A2k0

whose base change to k recovers Y .
    Step 2: Embedding into C.
    Because k0 is finitely generated over Q, there exists an embedding

                                            k0 ,→ C.

Base change along this embedding yields a finite étale morphism

                                           YC → A2C .

   Step 3: Comparison with complex topology.
   The analytification of A2C is
                                  C2 ,
which is simply connected.
    By the Riemann Existence Theorem (SGA 1, Exposé XII, Thm. 5.2), finite étale covers
of A2C correspond to finite topological covering spaces of C2 .
    Since C2 admits no nontrivial connected finite coverings, YC is a disjoint union of copies
of A2C .
   Step 4: Descent of triviality.
   Because k is algebraically closed, extension of algebraically closed base fields does not
change the étale fundamental group (SGA 1, Exposé XIII, Cor. 2.12).
   Hence the triviality of the finite étale cover over C implies the triviality over k.
   Therefore
                                         π1t (A2k ) = 0.
