Quasi-Finiteness
Since étale morphisms are locally quasi-finite ([15, Tag 02G4]), it follows that

                                       F : A2 −→ A2

is quasi-finite.
    Equivalently, every fiber of F consists of finitely many points, and the induced extension
of function fields is finite.
Lemma 12.2 (Regular functions on a dense open of A2 ). Let k be an algebraically closed
field. Let U ⊂ A2k be a dense open subset. Then every regular function on U extends
uniquely to a regular function on A2k . In particular,

                                       O(U ) = k[x, y].

Proof. The affine plane A2k is normal, since it is the spectrum of the polynomial ring k[x, y],
which is a UFD and hence integrally closed ([8, II.6.11]).
    Let f ∈ O(U ). Since U is dense and A2k is normal, regular functions on U extend
uniquely across codimension one subsets ([8, II.6.11]).
   Therefore f extends to a global regular function on A2k , which must be a polynomial in
k[x, y].
    Hence O(U ) = k[x, y].                                                                  ■

