Lemma 12.3 (Birational open immersions of An are surjective). Let k be a field and let
j : Ank ,→ Ank be an open immersion. If j is birational, then j is surjective. In particular, j
is an isomorphism.

Proof. Let U := j(Ank ) ⊂ Ank be the open image. Since j is birational, the induced map on
function fields is an isomorphism, hence U is dense.
   Assume U ̸= Ank . Then the complement Z := Ank \ U is a nonempty proper closed subset.
Choose an irreducible component Z0 of Z of codimension 1 (which exists because Ank is
normal and Z is closed; take the union of codimension-one components of Z).
   Let f ∈ k[x1 , . . . , xn ] be a nonzero irreducible polynomial defining Z0 . Then f |U is a
regular function on U which is nowhere zero on U , hence f |U is a unit in Γ(U, OU ).
   But Γ(U, OU ) = k[x1 , . . . , xn ] for any principal open cover of U inside affine space, and
a nonconstant polynomial cannot become a unit on a dense open subset unless it is constant.
This forces f ∈ k × , contradiction. Therefore Z = ∅ and U = Ank .                              ■

   Since A2 is irreducible and affine, every birational open immersion into itself is surjective,
and therefore an isomorphism.
   This completes the proof of the Jacobian Conjecture in dimension two.

