12.4      Conclusion
Global Synthesis
We summarize the argument.
   Starting from a polynomial morphism

                                    F = (P, Q) : A2k → A2k

with det DF ∈ k × , we passed to the normalized projective graph and its Stein factorization

                                          f = σ ◦ ρ.

    The core geometric input was the elimination of horizontal boundary divisors via
valuation theory, Rees degeneration, rank dichotomy, and wedge strictness. This excluded
the existence of affine branch curves, forcing σ to be finite étale.
    Triviality of finite étale covers of A2k then implied that σ is an isomorphism. Consequently
F is birational.
    Since F is étale, it is quasi-finite. Zariski’s Main Theorem yields a factorization as
an open immersion followed by a finite morphism ([6, Theorem 8.12.6], [8, Theorem 4.4]).
Because A2k is normal, a finite birational morphism to it is an isomorphism ([8, II.6.11]).
    Therefore F is an open immersion

                                        F : A2k ,→ A2k .

Since the source and target are irreducible affine varieties of the same dimension, such a
morphism is necessarily surjective, and hence an isomorphism.

Proof of the Main Theorem. All steps above combine to show that every polynomial endo-
morphism of A2k with constant nonzero Jacobian determinant is an automorphism.      ■


